p.src:
The same construction gives an inner product between two different four-vectors, $A\mathbin{\cdot}B=A_\mu B^\mu=A^\mu B_\mu.$ Its invariance follows without writing an explicit Lorentz matrix. Since $A+B$ and $A-B$ are four-vectors, both of their squared norms are invariant: $\begin{aligned}
  (A+B)^2&=A^2+B^2+2A\mathbin{\cdot}B,\\
  (A-B)^2&=A^2+B^2-2A\mathbin{\cdot}B.\end{aligned}$ Subtracting gives $(A+B)^2-(A-B)^2=4A\mathbin{\cdot}B,$ so $A\mathbin{\cdot}B$ is invariant as well. The factor of four follows directly from the expansion.

